  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align="right" valign="top" nowrap><b>Subject:</b></td>
    <td align="left" colspan="2" valign="top">gyroscopic particles</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td align=right class=medium valign="top" nowrap><b>Question:</b></td>
    <td align=left class=medium valign="top" width="400" colspan="2">In the documentary Free Energy - The Race To Zero Point Joseph Newman talks about how the actions/reactions of a gyroscope are exactly the same as when you move a conductor through a magnetic field.   He goes on to explain his theory that the energy in a magnetic field is made up of  "gyroscopic particles".  Does this make sense?   Is this anything new?   As a casual observer I have to agree that the feeling of moving a gyroscope sure does feel the same as moving two magnets against each other in different directions.   <br>
<br>
Just a thought.<br>
<br>
<br>
<br>
</td>
  </tr>
